the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n\n**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n\n**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** A monthly janitor deletes `.ard/<issue#>/` for issues closed more than 90 days ago; git history keeps them.\n\n## Scanners","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67c4bb85-4492-4174-b062-ed4f86ada1c4","uuid":"5a5ec62a-5b23-4c44-b666-4e33e3c37f89","timestamp":"2026-11-23T16:59:42.138Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\t}\n\treturn p\n}\n\n// registerLoops returns the loops to run: the scanners loop (#23), the stale\n//