 `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n\n**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n\n**Directories outlive their issues as the audit trail.** A monthly janitor deletes `.ard/<issue#>/` for issues closed more than 90 days ago; git history keeps them.\n\n## Scanners","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GLoz5qhbv5fq1YGzWsztt8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
